        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-precor-is" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. What Precor Actually Is</a>
          <a href="#peloton" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. The Peloton Connection</a>
          <a href="#why-not-new" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. Why You Probably Should Not Buy One New</a>
          <a href="#buying-used" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Buying One Used: The Route That Works</a>
          <a href="#living-with-one" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Living With a Commercial Machine at Home</a>
          <a href="#versus" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Precor vs the Other Commercial Brands</a>
          <a href="#who-should-buy" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Who Should Buy One</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Precor is not trying to sell you a treadmill. It sells to gyms, hotels, apartment
          buildings and universities, and it builds machines to survive that. For a home buyer
          that is not an obstacle — it is the reason the secondhand market is full of hardware you
          could not otherwise afford.
        </p>

        <div id="what-precor-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. What Precor Actually Is</h2>
<p>
            Precor has built commercial fitness equipment since 1980, out of Washington state, and
            its reputation rests on two things: machines that survive institutional use, and a
            genuine engineering record. The company developed the elliptical trainer as a commercial
            product, and its cushioning system — marketed as Ground Effects Impact Control — is one
            of the few deck-cushioning designs that is a real engineering approach rather than a
            marketing name for foam.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The treadmills are the TRM lines and the Experience and Assurance series.</strong>
            Numbering broadly tracks capability and console sophistication, and the machines are
            built around the same priorities throughout: a heavy steel frame, a motor rated for
            continuous commercial duty, a long deck, and a design that assumes a technician will
            open it periodically.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>That last point is the one that matters most to a home buyer.</strong> A
            consumer treadmill is designed to be assembled once and thrown away. A commercial
            treadmill is designed to be maintained, rebuilt, re-decked and kept in service for
            fifteen years while being used sixteen hours a day. Those are different engineering
            problems, and they produce different machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            The visible consequences are a frame that does not flex, a deck long enough that you
            stop thinking about it, a motor that never sounds like it is working, and a machine that
            is extremely heavy. The invisible consequence is that parts exist and the thing can be
            serviced, which is the property that gives a fifteen-year-old Precor more residual value
            than a five-year-old consumer machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="peloton" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Peloton Connection</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Peloton Interactive has owned Precor since 2021.</strong> The acquisition was
            announced at the end of 2020 and closed the following spring, and it was one of the
            larger deals in the sector.
          </p>
          <p>
            The logic was not really about treadmills. Peloton was constrained on manufacturing
            capacity and had almost no presence in the commercial channel — the hotels, apartment
            buildings and corporate gyms where people first encounter equipment brands. Precor came
            with US factories, an established dealer network and exactly that commercial footprint.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What it means for a buyer is mostly reassurance rather than change.</strong>
            Precor continues to operate as a commercial brand with its own product lines and its own
            service network, and the machines have not become
            <a href="/peloton-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Peloton Treads</a> with
            different badges. The two ranges serve different customers and remain distinct.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The useful implication is about the wider market.</strong> A home buyer weighing
            a Peloton against a used commercial machine is, on this one axis, choosing between two
            products of the same corporate parent — one built around a subscription and a
            touchscreen, the other around a fifteen-year service life and no software at all. Our
            <a href="/peloton-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Peloton guide</a> runs
            the five-year subscription arithmetic; set that total against what a used TRM costs and
            the comparison becomes genuinely interesting.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>One caveat worth holding.</strong> Corporate ownership changes strategy over
            time, and the commercial channel is a different business from direct-to-consumer. None
            of that affects a machine already built, but it is a reason to confirm current parts and
            service arrangements for an older unit rather than assuming continuity indefinitely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-not-new" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Why You Probably Should Not Buy One New</h2>
<p>
            Precor does sell into the home market, and the honest advice for most households is not
            to buy that way.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>New commercial pricing reflects a commercial denominator.</strong> These
            machines are priced against a service life measured in institutional hours. A gym
            running one sixteen hours a day amortises that easily. A household running one four
            hours a week is buying durability it will never consume — the same argument our
            <a href="/woodway-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Woodway guide</a>
            makes, and it applies here for the same reason.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A good consumer machine will outlast your interest in it.</strong> That is the
            uncomfortable truth underneath most premium home-fitness purchases. A well-maintained
            <a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole</a> or
            <a href="/horizon-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon</a> at a fraction
            of the price will still be running long after the reason you bought it has changed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The exceptions are real but narrow.</strong> If you are equipping a building —
            a small gym, an apartment amenity room, a corporate space, a physiotherapy practice —
            then you are the customer Precor is designed for, the dealer relationship has value, and
            buying new is the correct decision. Our
            <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial treadmill
            guide</a> covers what facility buying involves.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>For everyone else, the interesting Precor is the used one</strong>, and the rest
            of this page is about that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Buying One Used: The Route That Works</h2>
<p>
            The structural opportunity here is worth stating clearly, because it is not
            opportunism — it is how the commercial equipment market works.
          </p>
          <p>
            Facilities replace cardio equipment on capital cycles driven by depreciation schedules,
            refurbishment programmes, lease terms and the need to look current — not by machines
            wearing out. A hotel refitting its gym releases treadmills with years of service life
            remaining. Commercial resellers buy those in bulk, refurbish to varying standards, and
            sell on. Our
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new versus used
            analysis</a> makes the general case; on commercial brands the gap between secondhand
            price and remaining usefulness is wider than anywhere else in the category.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Three channels, in descending order of safety.</strong> A commercial
            remanufacturer will have replaced wear parts, will often offer a limited warranty, and
            will deliver — the most expensive route and by some distance the least risky. A used
            equipment dealer sells as-is or lightly serviced, cheaper, variable. A private or auction
            sale is cheapest and carries the whole risk, including that nobody will help you move
            400 lbs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What to check, beyond our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">standard
            inspection</a>:</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Deck and belt condition.</strong> Commercial decks are usually reversible and
            replaceable, so wear is a negotiating point rather than a disqualification — but
            establish which side is in use and whether it has already been flipped.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Hours, if the console reports them.</strong> Many commercial machines log
            running hours, which is far more informative than age. Ask.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Console function.</strong> Test everything. Older commercial consoles are the
            hardest parts to source and the most likely to be quietly dead.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Power requirements.</strong> The single biggest practical trap. Some commercial
            treadmills expect a supply a domestic room does not have. Establish the exact
            requirement from the model and serial before money changes hands, and involve an
            electrician if there is any doubt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="living-with-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Living With a Commercial Machine at Home</h2>
<p>
            The hardware is the easy part. The logistics are what catch people out, and they are
            worth thinking through before the machine exists rather than after.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Weight is the governing constraint.</strong> These do not fold, do not tilt
            helpfully and do not come apart for convenience. Measure the full route — doorway
            widths, stairwell turns, landing space, ceiling height at the top of the stairs — using
            the arithmetic in our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dimensions
            guide</a>, and price professional movers as part of the purchase. A ground floor or a
            garage is the realistic destination.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Floor loading matters</strong> in a way it does not with a consumer machine.
            A suspended floor carrying 400 lbs plus a running adult is worth a thought, and a
            basement slab or ground floor removes the question.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Noise is different rather than worse.</strong> The motor is generally quieter
            than a consumer machine because it is not working hard, but the mass transmits footfall
            efficiently into a structure. Our
            <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quiet treadmill guide</a>
            and <a href="/best-treadmills-for-apartments/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">apartment
            guide</a> cover what actually helps — and a
            <a href="/treadmill-mat/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">proper mat</a> matters more
            under a heavy machine than a light one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Find a technician before you need one.</strong> Commercial equipment is serviced
            by commercial technicians, and they exist in most areas because facilities need them.
            Establishing that relationship early is what turns a cheap used machine into a
            twenty-year one — and our
            <a href="/treadmill-repair/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">repair guide</a> covers
            how to find and evaluate one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Maintenance is ordinary.</strong> These are belt-over-deck machines, so
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubrication</a>
            applies exactly as it does elsewhere — check the manual, because some commercial decks
            have specific requirements and the wrong product is worse than none.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="versus" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Precor vs the Other Commercial Brands</h2>
<p>
            In the used market you will be choosing between a handful of names rather than a
            handful of models, so it helps to know how they differ.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Life Fitness</strong> is the closest direct comparison — similar market
            position, similar durability, similar availability secondhand, and arguably the widest
            parts network of any commercial brand. Our
            <a href="/life-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Life Fitness guide</a> and
            the <a href="/life-fitness-t3-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">T3 review</a>
            cover it. On hardware there is very little between them; buy whichever specific machine
            is in better condition.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Matrix</strong> is the Johnson Health Tech commercial brand, newer to the
            category than Precor but now very widely installed. See our
            <a href="/matrix-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Matrix guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Woodway</strong> is a different proposition entirely — slat belt, no
            lubrication, no deck to wear out, and priced accordingly even secondhand. Our
            <a href="/woodway-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Woodway guide</a>
            explains what that architecture buys.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The honest summary:</strong> among belt-and-deck commercial brands, the
            condition and history of the individual machine matters far more than the badge. A
            well-maintained Precor and a well-maintained Life Fitness will both outlast you. A
            neglected example of either is a project. Buy the machine, not the name — which is the
            opposite of the advice that applies in the consumer market, where the badge tells you
            about parts supply.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Who Should Buy One</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy used if you have the space and the access.</strong> A garage, a basement or
            a ground-floor room, a way to move 400 lbs, and a preference for owning one machine for
            twenty years rather than three in sequence. This is the case where a used Precor is
            genuinely one of the best purchases available in home fitness.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy used if you are a serious runner.</strong> Deck length, frame stability and
            motor headroom are all things a commercial machine gives you without argument, and our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">running
            guide</a> explains why those three specifications outrank everything else.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy new if you are equipping a building.</strong> You are the actual customer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not buy one if it has to go upstairs</strong> or onto a suspended floor you
            are unsure about. This constraint ends the conversation more often than price does.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not buy one if you mostly walk.</strong> Everything you would be paying for
            and moving is irrelevant at 3 mph. Our
            <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking picks</a>
            weigh a fraction as much and cost a fraction as much.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not buy one without confirming the electrical supply.</strong> It is the
            single most common way this purchase goes wrong, and it goes wrong after the machine is
            already in the building.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who owns Precor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Peloton Interactive. The acquisition was announced at the end of 2020 and closed the following spring. Peloton was constrained on manufacturing capacity and had little presence in the commercial channel, and Precor came with US factories, a dealer network and an established footprint in gyms and hotels. Precor continues to operate as a distinct commercial brand rather than being rebadged.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Precor treadmills good for home use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The hardware is excellent and the logistics are the problem. A used Precor gives you a heavy frame, a long deck and a motor that never works hard, for a fraction of new commercial pricing. But they do not fold, weigh several hundred pounds, need the right electrical supply, and realistically live on a ground floor, in a basement or in a garage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a new or used Precor treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Used, unless you are equipping a building. New commercial pricing reflects a service life measured in institutional hours, which a household will never consume. Facilities replace machines on capital cycles rather than because they are worn out, so the secondhand market carries units with years of life left at a small fraction of new.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I check when buying a used Precor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Beyond a normal used inspection: deck condition and whether it has already been flipped, logged running hours if the console reports them, every console function since older commercial displays are hardest to source, and — most importantly — the exact electrical requirement from the model and serial number. Confirm the supply before money changes hands, not after the machine is in your garage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Precor or Life Fitness — which is better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On hardware there is very little between them. Both build heavy, long-lived commercial machines with real parts networks, and both reach the used market in quantity from gym refits. The condition and maintenance history of the individual machine matters far more than the badge, so inspect both and buy whichever specific unit is in better shape.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you get parts for an old Precor treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally yes, which is a large part of why they are worth buying used. Commercial machines are designed to be maintained and rebuilt rather than discarded, and the dealer and technician network exists because facilities depend on it. Consoles on older units are the hardest component to source; frames, decks, belts, rollers and motors are far more straightforward.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Precor</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The first one ends the purchase after delivery, which is the worst possible moment to discover it.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not confirming the electrical requirement first</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Some commercial treadmills expect a supply a domestic room does not have, and this is discovered far too often once several hundred pounds of machine is already in the garage. Get the exact requirement from the model and serial number before agreeing a price, and have an electrician confirm the circuit if there is any doubt at all.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Underestimating the move</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">These do not fold, do not tilt helpfully and do not come apart for convenience. Measure the full route — doorway widths, stairwell turns, landing space, ceiling height — and price professional movers as part of the purchase rather than as an afterthought. If the destination is upstairs, that usually ends the conversation regardless of how good the deal is.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the badge instead of the machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">In the consumer market the brand tells you about parts supply, so it matters. In the used commercial market every serious badge has a parts network, and what varies is how the individual unit was maintained. A neglected Precor is a project and a well-serviced Life Fitness is a bargain. Inspect the machine in front of you and let the name come second.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming commercial means maintenance-free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">These are still belt-over-deck machines and still need lubricating, and a facility that skipped it has already spent some of the motor’s life. Check the manual rather than reaching for generic silicone, since some commercial decks have specific requirements and the wrong product is worse than none. Then find a technician before you need one.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

